('h2', "4.11  Communicating with Clinicians"),
('p', "Communication with a clinician has a different purpose: it must be **precise, structured, brief and complete**. The internationally recommended tool for clinical hand-off is **SBAR**, and it is examinable."),
('mne', ("\u201CSBAR\u201D \u2014 structured clinical communication (originally from the US Navy, now standard in health care)",
         ["**S \u2013 Situation:** who you are, who the patient is, and what the problem is in one sentence. *'This is Ramesh from Biochemistry. Regarding Mr Ram Lal, bed 12, ward 5 \u2014 I have a critical potassium result.'*",
          "**B \u2013 Background:** the relevant context. *'Sample received at 2.10 a.m. for renal profile; the patient is a known chronic kidney disease case.'*",
          "**A \u2013 Assessment:** the finding and its significance. *'Serum potassium is 7.2 mmol/L, confirmed on repeat; the sample was not haemolysed. This is in the critical range.'*",
          "**R \u2013 Recommendation / Request:** what you want to happen. *'Please review the patient urgently. Shall I keep the sample for a repeat, and may I have the name of the person receiving this result?'*",
          "Then close with a **read-back** and **document** the call: date, time, result, name of the person informed, and your own name."])),
('h3', "Rules for Communication with Clinicians and Colleagues"),
('b', [
    "**Be prepared** before you telephone: have the report, the identifiers and the previous value in front of you.",
    "**Identify yourself and your department** first; ask for and record the name and designation of the person you speak to.",
    "**Use exact figures with units** and state the reference range and the critical threshold.",
    "**Never use ambiguous abbreviations** or 'trailing zeros' in dosages when repeating verbal information.",
    "**Insist on a read-back** for every verbal or telephonic result and every verbal order.",
    "**Report analytical limitations honestly** \u2014 haemolysis, lipaemia, clot, insufficient volume, delay in transport, interference, dilution.",
    "**Escalate appropriately** \u2014 if the duty doctor cannot be reached, follow the written escalation ladder (senior resident \u2192 consultant \u2192 nursing supervisor \u2192 medical officer on call). **Never stop at one failed attempt.**",
    "**Do not offer a clinical diagnosis or treatment advice** beyond your role, but do offer the technical interpretation you are qualified to give.",
    "**Do not criticise a clinician in front of a patient**; raise disagreements privately and professionally.",
    "**Document everything** \u2014 in the critical-value register / LIS: date, time, result, informed to whom, read-back done, your signature.",
    "**Respect the chain of command** but never let hierarchy stop you from voicing a safety concern \u2014 use the **'CUS' words**: 'I am **C**oncerned \u2026 I am **U**ncomfortable \u2026 this is a **S**afety issue.'",
    "**Give feedback to the ward** on repeated pre-analytical errors, politely and with data, not with blame."
]),
('eg', ["**A model critical-value telephone call (learn this \u2014 it can be reproduced verbatim in the exam):**",
        "*'Good morning, this is Ramesh, laboratory technologist, Central Clinical Laboratory. May I know your name and designation, please? \u2026 Thank you, Sister Meena. I am calling about a critical result. Patient: Mr Ram Lal, UHID 45231, bed 12, male, 62 years. Serum potassium is seven point two millimoles per litre \u2014 that is seven, point, two. It has been confirmed on a repeat run and the sample was not haemolysed. This is above our critical limit of six point zero. Please inform the duty doctor immediately. Could you read the value back to me, please? \u2026 Yes, that is correct, seven point two. Thank you. I am recording this call at 2.35 a.m. and will retain the sample.'*"]),

('h2', "4.12  Communicating with Attendants and Relatives"),
('b', [
    "**Confirm identity and relationship** before disclosing anything; the patient's information belongs to the **patient**, not the family.",
    "**Take the patient's permission** (ideally recorded in advance) about who may be given information.",
    "**Talk in a private place**, never in the corridor or at the counter within earshot of others.",
    "**Address the senior-most responsible attendant** but include the person who will actually give care.",
    "**Use simple language**, avoid jargon, avoid numbers without explanation.",
    "**Do not give a diagnosis or prognosis** \u2014 that is the treating clinician's role. Say clearly: 'The report will be given to your doctor, who will explain what it means.' This single sentence prevents most conflicts.",
    "**Explain the process and the realistic time**: why the test takes six hours, why culture takes 48\u201372 hours.",
    "**Answer questions about charges and procedures** honestly.",
    "**Manage expectations proactively** \u2014 an attendant told 'it will take three hours' does not become an angry attendant at hour two.",
    "**Handle aggression safely** \u2014 stay calm, keep a safe distance and an exit available, call security when needed, and never argue with a group.",
    "**Show empathy for their anxiety** \u2014 they are frightened, not hostile by nature."
]),

('h2', "4.13  Documentation and Written Communication"),
('b', [
    "**Legible, in ink, no pencil**; no overwriting, no whitener \u2014 strike through a single line, write the correct entry, initial and date it.",
    "**Contemporaneous** \u2014 recorded at the time of the event, never back-dated.",
    "**Complete** \u2014 two patient identifiers, specimen type, collection date and time, receipt time, method, result with units and reference range, comments, and the signature and designation of the person releasing the report.",
    "**Accurate and objective** \u2014 record facts, not opinions about the patient ('patient was abusive' \u2192 better: 'patient declined the procedure and left at 10.15 a.m.').",
    "**Confidential** \u2014 records locked, screens locked, individual passwords never shared, reports not left on open counters.",
    "**Retained** for the period prescribed by law and by NABL/institutional policy.",
    "**Amendments** to an issued report must be made as a clearly marked **corrected report**, with the reason, and the original must not be destroyed; the clinician must be actively informed.",
    "**Remember the legal maxim:** *'Not documented, not done.'* In court, a good record is the professional's best defence."
]),

